e J3s, but unlikely.\n\nAnother idea: Reversible Tiles (12 points). The description says Reversible Tiles consist of tiles whose shape is point-symmetric. Available tiles: B1, B2, B3, B4, B5, B8, B9, T2, T4, T5, T6, T8, T9, J3.\n\nCheck current hand:\n\nW tiles: W1, W4, W5, W8, W9 \u2013 but W suits aren't in the reversible list (reversible only includes B, T, and J3). So W tiles disqualify this pattern.\n\nThus, Reversible Tiles is impossible because of W tiles.\n\nHow about All Types (6 points): contains all three suits, winds, and dragons. They have W, T, B suits, and J3 (dragon), but no wind tiles. Prevailing wind is South (F2), their seat wind is North (F4). If they can get a wind tile, but currently none. So maybe possible if they draw a wind, but not sure.\n\nAlternatively, Mixed Shifted Chows (6 points): three consecutive chows in different suits shifted by one. Not obvious.\n\nBack to Peng decision.\n\nIf they Peng W8:\n\n- Expose a Pung of W8. This gives them one pung. Remaining hand needs to form three more melds and a pair.\n\n- Loses the pair of W8, which could have been useful for pairs-based hands.\n\n- May help in patterns like All Pungs (6 points), but they need four pungs. With one exposed, they'd need three more. Possible but challenging.\n\n- Also, since it's an exposed pung (from Peng), it prevents Fully Concealed Hand (4 points) but they probably weren't going for that anyway.\n\nIf they don't Peng and pass:\n\n- Keep the pair of W8. Could aim for pairs-based hand like Seven Pairs (if they get more pairs), or use W8 pair as the eye.\n\n- Alternatively, try to form chows or other pungs.\n\nBut what's the immediate benefit? Let's see possible waits.\n\nCurrent hand has 13 tiles. After drawing a tile, they need to discard one. But in this case, the decision is whether to Peng the discarded W8 or not.\n\nAssuming they pass, they would draw a tile from the wall as usual. But the key is whether taking W8 now improves their chances.\n\nAnother conside